From 6ac8de7beb78d53e9ba1ab53e12f50705677a0e5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: JorreFog <JorreFog@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 20:15:59 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] ARMJIT_A64: fewer host instructions per block and per exit

Each change is on by default and exact (same cycles, memory and pictures). MELONDS_JIT_OLDCODEGEN=1
turns all of them off (the code is then what patches 0001-0006 generate); MELONDS_JIT_NO<name>=1
turns off one. All are read once, when the compiler is created.

- FASTENTRY: the fast memory base (x26) is set by a four-instruction prologue at each block's
  entry, which the runtime dispatches to; link slots and DynExit enter past it (x26 is callee-saved
  and only blocks of the same CPU set it), so a linked block doesn't reload it.
- CONDEXIT: a conditional branch with a constant target ending a block gets a known exit each way
  (ExitCheck and a link slot), instead of DynExit's lookup; the condition is checked again on the
  host's flags or RCPSR.
- MEMADDR: a 16/32-bit [rn] fast access uses rn as the address instead of a copy in w0; a patched
  (faulted) access moves it to w0 before its call (LoadStorePatch::AddrReg).
- CODESTORES: ARM7 CodeRegion/CodeCycles in one 64-bit store; a constant jump skips storing
  CodeRegion/CodeCycles (ARM7) or RegionCodeCycles (ARM9) when the block already stored the same
  values on every path to it.
- DEAD: no CLS/CLZ before ARM7 multiplies (Comp_AddCycles_CI ignores the register), no ARM9
  CodeCycles store before compiled instructions (only the interpreter reads it), no R15 load into a
  host register for a conditional B/BL.
- EXITSTUB: ExitCheck/DynExit add the cycles with one sxtw-extended add, return to ARM_Ret through a
  literal, and come in a variant without the CPSR store for blocks that left RCPSR as it is in
  memory; DynExit computes the lookup address in fewer instructions and loads the code base from a
  literal.
- EXITPC: a constant exit's R15 is stored by the stub (ExitCheckPC, from a literal word after the
  link slot) instead of a mov/movk/str in the block; so is the not-taken exit's of a conditional
  immediate branch ending a block (no R15 store before it).
- SELFLOOP: an exit back to the block's own start, when nothing in the block stores R15 (so memory
  still has the block's), does ExitCheck's work inline and goes round through a link slot linked
  from the start (UnlinkBlock unlinks it as any other); back to the loop stores R15.
- LOOPREGS: such a loop block (only compiled instructions, no block transfers, MSR/MRS, register
  jumps or R15 operands, no branch followed when taken) loads every guest register it uses once at
  its entry and keeps them in host registers round the loop (no unloading; slow memory calls save
  and reload caller-saved ones); every way out stores them all.
  A Thumb PUSH/POP/LDMIA/STMIA of one register (not PC) qualifies: with the register loaded it is
  Comp_MemAccessBlock's single access.
- KEEPFLAGS: the functions a faulted fast memory access is patched to call keep the host's NZCV,
  so a fast access no longer ends the host flags' validity (conditions after loads branch on them).
- FOLDEXIT: a conditional immediate branch ending a block has its taken way's exit inside the
  branch (stores, one cycle add of its jump cycles plus the block's constant ones, known by then:
  the not-taken way's are the last) and its other way's at the end, instead of a join and a second
  condition check.
- LAZYFLAGS: in a LOOPREGS block, an unconditional instruction's four flags stay in the host's NZCV
  (PendingFlags) until something would change or read NZCV/RCPSR (the emitter calls FlagsTouched
  first: Write32 for an NZCV writer or an instruction naming w27, BL before taking its offset), an
  instruction not known safe starts, or an exit, which puts them in RCPSR on its way only. A Thumb
  ADD/SUB/CMP/CMN/NEG that sets two or more flags drops pending ones (it overwrites all four the
  same way first). When the loop puts all four flags in RCPSR before reading any, the way round
  leaves them and the CPSR store out; the loop's check then compares Timestamp and Target by the sign
  of their difference (no NZCV write), so its ways out still find the flags in NZCV. A callee-saved
  host register the loop's guest registers leave free holds the Timestamp's address.

MELONDS_JIT_PERFMAP also names the shared stubs in the map (stub_e9xx/e7xx JumpTo, ec0n ExitCheck,
ed0n DynExit, eb00 the rest), and MELONDS_JIT_PERFDIR puts the map and code dump elsewhere than /tmp.
